    "sourceWikitext": "{{Recipe summary\n| Category = Flatbread recipes\n| Difficulty = 3\n}}\n\n{{Recipe}}\n\n== Ingredients ==\n* 1 [[Cookbook:Tsp|tsp]] dried active [[Cookbook:Yeast|yeast]]\n* 1 tsp white granulated [[Cookbook:Sugar|sugar]]\n* 200 [[Cookbook:Gram|g]] (7 [[Cookbook:Oz|oz]]) white bread [[Cookbook:Flour|flour]]\n* 1 [[Cookbook:Pinch|pinch]] black onion ([[Cookbook:Nigella|nigella]]) seeds\n* ¼ tsp [[Cookbook:Salt|salt]]\n* ½ tsp [[Cookbook:Baking Powder|baking powder]]\n* 1 [[Cookbook:Tbsp|tbsp]] [[Cookbook:Vegetable oil|vegetable oil]]\n* 2 tbsp natural (plain) [[Cookbook:Yogurt|yoghurt]]\n* 2 tbsp [[Cookbook:Milk|milk]]\n\n== Procedure ==\n# Preheat the [[Cookbook:Oven|oven]] to 140°C/275°F/Gas 1.\n# In a small bowl, mix the yeast with 1 tablespoon of warm water. Stir in the sugar and leave it in a warm place for 5 minutes until the yeast is covered with froth. This will show that the yeast is ready to use.\n# Mix together the flour, onion seeds, salt and baking powder. Stir in the oil, yoghurt and milk, then stir in the activated yeast mixture.\n# To [[Cookbook:Kneading|knead]] the dough, clench your hand into a fist, wet your knuckles with a little water, then press them repeatedly into the dough. Continue pressing and kneading until you have a soft, pliable dough. It should take about 10 minutes. Leave to rest for 10 minutes and then knead again for another 5 minutes.\n# Place the dough in a mixing bowl, cover it with [[Cookbook:Plastic Wrap|cling film]] and leave it in a warm place to rise for 20–30 minutes.\n# Divide the dough into four balls and place on a floured surface or board. [[Cookbook:Dough#Rolling|Roll]] each into a long oval shape about 0.5 cm (¼ inch) thick.\n# Place them on a greased [[Cookbook:Baking Sheet|baking tray]] and put them in the centre of the oven for 10–15 minutes. They are ready when they have puffed up a little. Serve fresh from the oven.\n\n== Notes, tips, and variations ==\n* To add an extra crispy finish, pop the baked naans under a preheated grill for 30 seconds before brushing with butter and serving.\n* To add extra flavour, consider adding finely chopped garlic and coriander to the butter you brush over your cooked naan breads. Then return them to the oven (or place under a grill) for a short time to cook the garlic.\n[[Category:Recipes using baking powder]]\n[[Category:Recipes using white sugar]]\n[[Category:Recipes for flatbread]]\n[[Category:Recipes using bread flour]]\n[[Category:Recipes using vegetable oil]]\n[[Category:Recipes using milk]]",
